  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 136 PM CDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nUffda, what a beautiful afternoon! Temperatures ranging mostly in \nthe 60s although a few lower 70s are being reported for sites \nsouthwest of the MN River Valley. A 1035mb surface high pressure \nover the northeastern Great Lakes region has allowed for continued \nsubsidence this afternoon. Skies will continue to be essentially \ncloud-free with forecast highs topping out in the upper 60s to near \n70 degrees. Winds will continue to be out of the south this \nafternoon with few gusts nearing 20 to 25 mph along mainly for \nwestern MN. This area of high pressure is expected to depart to\nthe east this evening, while a troughing pattern moves in \nacross the Canadian Prairies tonight. As of now, the troughing \naxis is well displaced to our north to serve as any concern for \nprecip chances. As we head into Wednesday, subsidence will \nremain in place however not as prevalent as today's environment.\nTherefore, portions of central MN could see some cloud \ndevelopment as a result. Areas south can expect mostly sunny \nconditions. Once again had to nudge MaxTs up using NBM90th \npercentile allowing for highs to reach the upper 60s to lower \n70s. Thursday's highs will be even warmer and run practically 15\nto 20 degrees above season climatology. Most areas can expect \nwidespread 70s and a few isolated sites nearing 80 degrees near \nthe Buffalo Ridge. \n\nA pattern shift arrives later this week as low pressure moves \nfurther inland from the western CONUS. This feature is then \ncarried northward by the strong ridging across the south-central\nUS. The low becomes fully developed by the time its carried \nover the Canadian border and converges with a deepening trough \nover British Columbia. Once again, it looks like we will be too \nfar displaced from the strongest forcing by the time it nears \nthe northern plains. The region is then left with a trailing \ncold front that moves through Friday into early this weekend. \nFelt the NBM represents PoPs well with portions of west-central \nMN seeing 40 PoPs Friday afternoon then shifting eastward \nacross the remaining forecast area Saturday. Although PoPs are \nincreased, QPF is on the lower end with about a tenth or two of \nan inch possible. Temperatures will be noticeably cooler late \nthis weekend into next week with highs only getting into the 50s\nand lows in the lower 40s to upper 30s.",
